{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And you shall remember that you were a slave.</b> The verse here explains the reason for why one's male and female slave rest — because through this, you and they labor all six days of work, and on the seventh they rest; you shall remember that you were a slave like them in Egypt, and YHWH redeemed you. But in the first Decalogue it did not explain the reason, because He did not wish to mention their disgrace at their moment of rejoicing. And even though it is written in the first ones, ״אשר הוצאתיך מארץ מצרים״, nonetheless He mentioned it in a manner of honor. Another interpretation: <b>And you shall remember that you were a slave</b> in Egypt, and the Holy One, blessed be He, redeemed you from there for rest; therefore your male slave and female slave shall rest like you." "<b>For you were a slave in the land of Egypt.</b> And in the first Decalogue it is written ״כי ששת ימים עשה ה׳ את השמים״ etc. Therefore the Sages established in the Kiddush of Shabbat over the cup a remembrance of the work of Creation, as written in the second Decalogue. But for occasions not fixed as a remembrance of Creation, one says in them only a remembrance of the Exodus from Egypt." "<b>Therefore.</b> Because the Holy One, blessed be He, desires that you remember <b>that you were a slave.</b>"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:51:53.472583999Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}